    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d309">

                <pb facs="0369" n="1203" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1203"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/158</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-16T20:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 16, 1918, 8 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6274. Following for Sheldon from War Trade Board:</p>
                <p>Our No. 17. Your 8242. We wish to impress upon you that the licensing for Sweden
                    of commodities in quantities suggested pending final agreement is contrary to
                    policy approved by British and continuously followed heretofore by this Board.
                    Also that it will be considered by other northern neutrals as discriminating in
                    Sweden’s favor and will probably make all our negotiations more difficult. If
                    after weighing these disadvantages Allied representatives feel the concession
                    necessary we authorize you to make the arrangement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
